Prepare a change impact report for @pnpm/parse-wanted-dependency. We propose making its parser always return both alias and bareSpecifier and requiring both fields in its named result types. Do not implement the change. Determine which existing internal consumers and package validation targets need review.

Affected means a direct static use or reexport of parseWantedDependency, ParseWantedDependencyResult or ParsedWantedDependency outside the defining package. It does not mean that a test is proven to fail. Enumerate configured production source files under workspace package src directories, respecting each native TypeScript configuration. Exclude tests, declarations generated by the compiler, dependencies and source excluded by configuration from that direct set. Classify each file/export pair as runtime, type-only or reexport. A file may have multiple roles. Preserve the original export identity when an import is renamed. Do not equate a similarly named local wrapper with the public export.

Separately enumerate all installed workspace package dependents reachable through declared dependency, dev dependency, optional dependency or peer dependency edges. Include a concrete dependency chain for each. This is a conservative review set, not proof that every dependent uses the symbol. Keep exact package versions and actual workspace versus external package identity.

List the existing compile, test and _test package.json scripts for the target package and packages owning direct source consumers. If all such packages use centralized scripts instead, list the root check, test-unit and build scripts that actually exist. These are candidate validation targets, not claims that they were executed. Identify the similarly named local wrapper as an exclusion from target symbol identity, even if its file contains a real call. Explicitly state limits for external consumers, computed loading, package reachability and unexecuted validation.

Write impact-answer.json in the repository root using the supplied JSON schema. Quotes must be exact source evidence with one-based line ranges of at most 21 lines. Use only negativeClaims that are independently supported exclusions. Leave every existing source, dependency, test and configuration file unchanged. Source discovery and analysis method are up to you.

Answer schema (illustrative values, not expected facts):
